msgid "ai_ad_transparency.body"
msgstr ""
"<p>The more polished an advert looks, the more readily we may assume that"
" the product must look exactly the same. Generative AI now makes it quick"
" to replace a background, refine a person or create a scene that was "
"never photographed. That is not a reason to treat every AI-assisted "
"advert as suspect. It is a reason to separate an image’s persuasive "
"effect from the facts that can support a buying decision. Let a striking "
"visual catch your attention, then let a second, calmer check decide "
"whether you purchase, subscribe or share your details.</p><h3>An AI "
"disclosure is not a danger label</h3><p>AI-assisted advertising can help "
"small businesses explain an idea without the cost of a large shoot. "
"Knowing that AI was used does not, by itself, tell you whether a product "
"is good or a claim is true. The real risk is mistaking an edited scene "
"for a product promise. On 9 July 2026, Google announced expanded "
"disclosures in Search, YouTube and Discover through a “How this ad was "
"made” panel, intended to make AI creation or editing easier to "
"understand. Availability and placement can change, but the useful habit "
"is stable: look for context before you draw a conclusion from a "
"picture.</p><h3>A 30-second check before buying</h3><p><strong>First, "
"open the disclosure.</strong> If an ad menu offers ad information or a "
"creation notice, use it to understand the image—not to pass judgement on "
"the advertiser. <strong>Next, leave the ad to verify the seller.</strong>"
" Find the company name, contact route, returns policy and terms on the "
"seller’s own site. Compare them with search results, map listings and "
"independent reviews; unexplained differences in name or address are worth"
" pausing over. <strong>Finally, read the promise in words.</strong> Check"
" price, delivery, recurring charges, cancellation, warranty and "
"eligibility in the product page or checkout text. The stronger the "
"phrase—“limited,” “lowest price,” “guaranteed”—the more carefully its "
"conditions and evidence deserve to be read.</p><h3>Move your attention "
"from the image to the evidence</h3><p>Odd hands, impossible shadows or "
"broken lettering can sometimes reveal a synthetic image. But spotting "
"visual flaws is not the goal. Convincing images and ordinary photographs "
"alike can leave out material details. When an offer matters, move beyond "
"the image to the specification, customer photos, support contact and the "
"exact return or cancellation path. Treat health, beauty, investment, "
"employment and child-related ads with extra care: an ad should begin "
"research, not end it.</p><h3>Use transparency as room to think</h3><p>A "
"disclosure about AI use is not a verdict on an advertiser. It gives you a"
" small pause in which to ask: what is presentation, and what can I "
"independently confirm? That pause protects your ability to choose. You do"
" not need to reject a beautiful ad; you only need to check the seller, "
"the conditions and the supporting evidence before pressing the "
"button.</p><h3>Current source consulted</h3><p><a "
"href=\"https://blog.google/products/ads-commerce/google-ads-ai-"
"transparency-labels/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener "
"noreferrer\">Google: Expanding AI transparency in ads</a>, published 9 "
"July 2026. This article independently develops a consumer-facing "
"verification routine from that announcement.</p>"

msgid "ai_ad_transparency.description"
msgstr ""
"A practical guide to using an ad’s creation disclosure to check its "
"source, claims and purchase conditions before you act."

msgid "ai_ad_transparency.heading"
msgstr "What to check first when an ad may use AI"

msgid "ai_ad_transparency.keywords"
msgstr ""
"AI-generated ads, AI advertising, ad transparency, how an ad was made, "
"purchase check, digital literacy"

msgid "ai_ad_transparency.title"
msgstr "How to Read AI-Generated Ads: A Practical Guide to Transparency Notices"

msgid "ai_live_translation.body"
msgstr ""
"<p>Hearing another language in your own language almost as it is spoken "
"is no longer a travel-only novelty. Live AI translation can open up a "
"call with an overseas client, a group project with an exchange student, "
"or a conversation at a hotel or clinic. But the smoother a translation "
"sounds, the easier it is to mistake it for certainty. A single word can "
"change a name, price, date, consent or health-related instruction. Use AI"
" as a way for both people to check meaning, not as an interpreter to "
"which the conversation is blindly handed over.</p><h3>Translation is "
"moving into the conversation itself</h3><p>On 1 July 2026, Google "
"presented Gemini 3.5 Live Translate in its June AI roundup. Its "
"announcement describes speech-to-speech translation that can "
"automatically detect more than 70 languages, retain natural intonation "
"and reduce awkward pauses, with rollout through the Gemini Live API, "
"Google AI Studio and Google Translate. Alongside Android 17 and local "
"models, the larger shift is clear: translation is moving from a copy-and-"
"wait task into live meetings and everyday movement. Availability by "
"language, country and device can change, so check the official status "
"before relying on it.</p><h3>Five rules that make it "
"useful</h3><p><strong>1. State the purpose in one sentence.</strong> "
"Confirm a booking, agree an owner and deadline, or ask a class question. "
"<strong>2. Prepare names and numbers.</strong> Keep proper names, "
"acronyms, places and amounts visible because noise and pronunciation "
"affect recognition. <strong>3. Keep each sentence to one idea.</strong> "
"Read key replies back: “So the deadline is Tuesday at 3 p.m., correct?” "
"Avoid idioms, irony and vague subjects; add the subject and rephrase if "
"needed. <strong>4. Confirm important matters in writing.</strong> For "
"contracts, payments, healthcare, safety or hiring, check amounts, "
"currencies, dates and conditions on screen or paper and have both sides "
"repeat them. A translation is a draft for shared understanding, not a "
"formal record. <strong>5. Know where the audio goes.</strong> Check "
"whether processing is on-device or on a server, whether recordings or "
"history remain, and whether model-training settings can be changed. Use "
"headphones in public and keep confidential conversations in approved "
"services.</p><h3>A dialogue tool, not a perfect interpreter</h3><p>The "
"value of live translation is not that it automates every word. It gives "
"people without a shared language room to speak first, check meaning and "
"ask again. Set the purpose, speak briefly, read back what matters and "
"understand how audio is handled. An awkward output is not a failure; it "
"is a cue to slow down and build meaning together.</p><h3>Current source "
"consulted</h3><p><a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-"
"ai/technology/ai/google-ai-updates-june-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" "
"rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Google: AI updates announced in June "
"2026</a>, published 1 July 2026. This article independently develops "
"practical guidance on verification and privacy from the capabilities "
"described in that announcement.</p>"

msgid "ai_live_translation.description"
msgstr ""
"Using the latest Gemini 3.5 Live Translate and Android 17 news as a "
"starting point, this guide explains how to use live AI translation "
"accurately and responsibly in meetings, travel and learning."

msgid "ai_live_translation.heading"
msgstr "Make live AI translation a useful part of the conversation"

msgid "ai_live_translation.keywords"
msgstr ""
"live AI translation, real-time voice translation, Gemini Live Translate, "
"multilingual meetings, translation AI"

msgid "ai_live_translation.title"
msgstr ""
"A Practical Guide to Live AI Translation: Five Rules for Meetings, Travel"
" and Learning"

