msgid "client_file_handoff.after_body"
msgstr ""
"A sent status does not prove that the recipient could review the file. "
"For deadline-sensitive material or files likely to be revised, ask for a "
"brief acknowledgement and keep a record for important exchanges. When the"
" sharing window ends, move the final version that must be retained to "
"your organisation’s designated storage and review the expiry or deletion "
"policy for the shared copy. The workflow remains the same with a browser-"
"based service such as FS!QR: check before sending, name files clearly, "
"confirm receipt, and tidy up afterwards. Those small habits make file "
"sharing easier and more reassuring for the client as well."

msgid "client_file_handoff.after_heading"
msgstr "After sending, confirm receipt and tidy up when the sharing window ends"

msgid "client_file_handoff.before_body"
msgstr ""
"Before creating a share link or attachment, state the recipient, the "
"file, and the review deadline in one sentence. For example: “Send the "
"July quotation PDF to Company A’s finance contact for review by 15 July.”"
" That reduces both misdirected files and the inevitable “which document "
"do you mean?” reply. If several people are involved internally, name the "
"sender and the person who approved the final version as well. Then use a "
"consistent filename order for the date, project, purpose, and version. A "
"filename is not mere housekeeping; it is the first safeguard against "
"opening the wrong document."

msgid "client_file_handoff.before_heading"
msgstr "Before sharing, put who receives what and by when in one sentence"

msgid "client_file_handoff.description"
msgstr ""
"Practical rules for sharing documents, images, and quotations with "
"clients while reducing misdirected files and needless follow-ups — before"
" sharing, while access is open, and after receipt."

msgid "client_file_handoff.heading"
msgstr "A Practical Checklist for Clear Client File Handoffs"

msgid "client_file_handoff.introduction"
msgstr ""
"When you send a file to a client, the job is not finished just because it"
" left your outbox. A handoff is complete only when the recipient can tell"
" exactly which document to review, how to open it, and by when. "
"Quotations, proposals, photos, and design files can cause confusion not "
"only through their contents, but also through the wrong version, the "
"wrong recipient, or an expired link. Here are the practical basics to put"
" in place before relying on any particular tool."

msgid "client_file_handoff.keywords"
msgstr ""
"client file sharing, send files to clients, secure sharing, prevent "
"misdirected files, sharing link, document delivery"

msgid "client_file_handoff.message_body"
msgstr ""
"A link by itself does not tell the recipient what the file is, whether "
"they need to act, or how long it will remain available. Include the "
"purpose of the share, the file or content to review, and the deadline for"
" using it or replying. Where appropriate, you may send access details "
"such as a password through a separate channel. The point is not to split "
"information for its own sake; it is to agree on a process that lets "
"recipients open the file safely without friction while making unintended "
"onward sharing less likely."

msgid "client_file_handoff.message_heading"
msgstr "Add three things to a share message besides the link"

msgid "client_file_handoff.title"
msgstr ""
"Client File Sharing Without Confusion: A Practical Checklist for Secure "
"Handoffs"

